.TH GIT-INLAY 1 "" "git-inlay" "Git Manual"
.SH NAME
git-inlay \- keep private files tracked and in a repository's tree, uncommitted to it
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
\fBgit inlay init\fR <name> [\-\-remote <url>] [\-\-no\-hooks]
\fBgit inlay clone\fR <url> [<name>] [\-\-no\-hooks]
\fBgit inlay attach\fR (<name>... | \-\-all) [\-\-no\-hooks]
\fBgit inlay detach\fR <name> [\-\-force] [\-\-keep\-files]
\fBgit inlay claim\fR <name> [\-\-] <path>...
\fBgit inlay unclaim\fR <name> [\-\-] <path>...
\fBgit inlay ls\fR [<name>] [\-\-porcelain]
\fBgit inlay which\fR <path>...
\fBgit inlay status\fR [<name>] [\-\-fetch] [\-\-porcelain]
\fBgit inlay check\fR [\-\-staged] [\-\-fix]
\fBgit inlay restore\fR [<name>]
\fBgit inlay prune\fR [\-\-dry\-run] [\-\-force]
\fBgit inlay hooks\fR (install [\-\-auto\-attach] | uninstall | status)
\fBgit inlay\fR <name> <git command> [<args>...]
\fBgit inlay version\fR
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
An \fIinlay\fR is a second git repository whose working tree is the
host's: the repository this command runs in. It owns a set of paths, its
\fIclaims\fR, recorded in \fI.git\-inlay/<name>.paths\fR, which the inlay
itself tracks. The host ignores every claim through a managed block in
\fI.git/info/exclude\fR, the inlay ignores everything else, and hooks on
both sides stop anything from crossing over. The inlay's git directory
lives under the host's \fI.git/inlays/\fR.
.PP
Every command but \fBversion\fR, \fBcheck\fR without \fB\-\-fix\fR
and the \fBhook\fR family first regenerates the two exclude files from
the claims, so they are always current.
.PP
\fBclaim\fR, \fBunclaim\fR and \fBwhich\fR read paths relative to the
current directory; a path that starts with \fB:(top)\fR is relative to
the root of the worktree instead.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
\fBinit\fR <name> [\-\-remote <url>] [\-\-no\-hooks]
Create an empty inlay for this worktree, with \fIurl\fR as its
\fBorigin\fR when given. A remote that is one of the host's is refused.
A name is made of lowercase letters, digits, and the characters
\fB.\fR, \fB_\fR and \fB\-\fR, and starts with a letter or a digit.
.TP
\fBclone\fR <url> [<name>] [\-\-no\-hooks]
Attach an inlay from its remote and check out its files. A file already
at a path it would write is never overwritten: the clone is refused.
With \fIname\fR, refuse a repository that holds a different inlay.
.TP
\fBattach\fR (<name>... | \-\-all) [\-\-no\-hooks]
Copy inlays attached in another worktree of the host into this one, on
the branch they have there and with the same remote. Only commits
travel.
.TP
\fBdetach\fR <name> [\-\-force] [\-\-keep\-files]
Remove an inlay from this worktree, and its tracked files from the tree
unless \fB\-\-keep\-files\fR is given. Uncommitted changes, stashes, and
commits on no remote stop it unless \fB\-\-force\fR is given.
.TP
\fBclaim\fR <name> [\-\-] <path>...
Give paths to an inlay and stage the files there. A path the host
tracks, or one that another inlay claims, is refused. A path that does
not exist yet is claimed as a file unless it ends with a slash.
.TP
\fBunclaim\fR <name> [\-\-] <path>...
Take paths back from an inlay. The files stay on disk and become
visible to the host as untracked.
.TP
\fBls\fR [<name>] [\-\-porcelain]
List claims, one \fIname\fR<TAB>\fIclaim\fR line each.
.TP
\fBwhich\fR <path>...
Print the owner of each path: an inlay's name, \fBhost\fR, or
\fBnone\fR.
.TP
\fBstatus\fR [<name>] [\-\-fetch] [\-\-porcelain]
Print each inlay's branch, its distance from its upstream, and its
counts of changed and untracked files. \fB\-\-fetch\fR fetches first.
.TP
\fBcheck\fR [\-\-staged] [\-\-fix]
Verify every invariant and exit 1 on a problem, naming the path, the
owner and the fix. It writes nothing, unless \fB\-\-fix\fR is given:
then it first regenerates the exclude files and repairs the inlays'
configuration. \fB\-\-staged\fR checks only what is staged in the host.
.TP
\fBrestore\fR [<name>]
Check out again the tracked inlay files that are missing from the tree,
as after \fBgit clean \-x\fR in the host. Files that are present are
left alone.
.TP
\fBprune\fR [\-\-dry\-run] [\-\-force]
Remove the inlays of worktrees that no longer exist. Those with commits
on no remote, or with stashes, are kept unless \fB\-\-force\fR is given.
.TP
\fBhooks\fR (install [\-\-auto\-attach] | uninstall | status)
Manage the host's guards: a \fBpre\-commit\fR hook that refuses a commit
which stages a claimed path, and a \fBpre\-push\fR hook that refuses a
push whose commits touch one. \fBinit\fR, \fBclone\fR and \fBattach\fR
install them wherever the host's hooks directory has room, unless given
\fB\-\-no\-hooks\fR. Where a hook manager owns \fBcore.hooksPath\fR, or
a hook of the host's own is in the way, \fBhooks install\fR adds them as
hooks defined in the repository's configuration, which needs git 2.54,
and otherwise prints the lines to add by hand. \fBhooks install
\-\-auto\-attach\fR also adds a \fBpost\-checkout\fR hook that runs
\fBattach \-\-all\fR in each worktree that \fBgit worktree add\fR
creates and checks out.
.TP
\fBhook session\-start\fR
Print a briefing on this worktree's inlays for a coding agent's
session\-start hook, or nothing where there are none. The other
\fBhook\fR events are what the installed hooks call.
.TP
\fI<name>\fR \fI<git command>\fR [\fI<args>\fR...]
Run any git command against an inlay, in the current directory.
\fBclean \-x\fR, \fBclean \-X\fR, \fBclean \-e '!...'\fR and
\fBstash \-\-all\fR (or \fB\-a\fR) are refused:
to an inlay the whole host tree is ignored files. \fBclean\fR and
\fBstash \-u\fR are refused while the inlay sees host files as
untracked, which a \fB.gitignore\fR pattern that re\-includes them
causes. The exit status is git's own.
.TP
\fBversion\fR
Print the version of git\-inlay and of git.
\fBgit inlay \-\-version\fR does the same.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
0
Success.
.TP
1
The command refused or failed: a guard or \fBcheck\fR found a problem,
an inlay is unknown, work would be lost, or files are in the way.
.TP
2
A usage error.
.TP
3
Git failed.
.SH FILES
.TP
\fI.git\-inlay/<name>.paths\fR
The claims of an inlay, one per line; a directory ends with a slash.
Tracked by the inlay, ignored by the host.
.TP
\fI$GIT_COMMON_DIR/inlays/\fR
The git directories of the inlays, per worktree.
.TP
\fI$GIT_COMMON_DIR/info/exclude\fR
Holds the managed block that makes the host ignore every claim.
.TP
\fI$GIT_COMMON_DIR/hooks/pre\-commit\fR, \fIpre\-push\fR, \fIpost\-checkout\fR
The shims that \fBhooks install\fR writes where the hooks directory has
room. Where it has none, on git 2.54 or newer, the guards are the
sections \fBhook.git\-inlay\-pre\-commit\fR, \fBhook.git\-inlay\-pre\-push\fR
and \fBhook.git\-inlay\-post\-checkout\fR of the host's
\fI.git/config\fR.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
\fBPRE_COMMIT_FROM_REF\fR, \fBPRE_COMMIT_TO_REF\fR, \fBPRE_COMMIT_REMOTE_NAME\fR, \fBPRE_COMMIT_LOCAL_BRANCH\fR
Set by the pre\-commit framework for a pre\-push hook. \fBgit\-inlay
hook pre\-push\fR reads the commits being pushed from them when they are
set, and from its standard input, as git supplies them, otherwise.
.SH NOTES
\fBgit inlay \-\-help\fR is answered by git, which shows this page;
\fBgit inlay \-h\fR prints the usage summary without it.
.SH SEE ALSO
\fBgit\fR(1), \fBgitignore\fR(5), \fBgithooks\fR(5)
.PP
https://github.com/acycl/git\-inlay
